00:03:09.714 --> 00:03:13.373
- Confidence secret number one
is eliminate negative people

00:03:13.373 --> 00:03:14.658
from your life.

00:03:14.658 --> 00:03:17.219
Negativity is more contagious than crabs.

00:03:17.219 --> 00:03:20.114
When you surround yourself
or have negative little

00:03:20.114 --> 00:03:22.190
cancerous people in your life,

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:25.573
they directly impact your
confidence in a negative way.

00:03:25.573 --> 00:03:26.742
Negative nasty people.

00:03:26.742 --> 00:03:27.830
Nasty.

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:29.939
Not like good nasty, bad nasty.

00:03:29.939 --> 00:03:32.087
As in get the freak away from me,

00:03:32.087 --> 00:03:33.581
I want nothing to do with you.

00:03:33.581 --> 00:03:35.696
Now, here's the issue,
when I say, hey, you just

00:03:35.696 --> 00:03:37.139
get rid of negative people.

00:03:37.139 --> 00:03:38.511
It's like poof, they're gone, right?

00:03:38.511 --> 00:03:39.541
Wrong.

00:03:39.541 --> 00:03:42.146
Because a lot of times, we
have these negative people,

00:03:42.146 --> 00:03:44.876
or these energy vampires all around us.

00:03:44.876 --> 00:03:46.810
They might be at work,
they might be at school,

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:49.077
they might actually be related to us.

00:03:49.077 --> 00:03:51.113
Does this mean that you
may need to find a new job?

00:03:51.113 --> 00:03:52.144
Yeah.

00:03:52.144 --> 00:03:53.303
Maybe a new relationship?

00:03:53.303 --> 00:03:54.668
Mm, quite possibly.

00:03:54.668 --> 00:03:56.733
Is it going to suck
for a little while now?

00:03:56.733 --> 00:03:57.726
Yes.

00:03:57.726 --> 00:03:59.269
In the long run is it
going to pay off big time

00:03:59.269 --> 00:04:00.631
in terms of your confidence?

00:04:00.631 --> 00:04:01.624
Yes.

00:04:01.624 --> 00:04:04.585
- Psychologists often call it
the confidence competence loop

00:04:04.585 --> 00:04:07.226
and as you become more
competent in something,

00:04:07.226 --> 00:04:09.624
you learn how to do it, you're
more confident in doing it.

00:04:09.624 --> 00:04:11.693
As you learn how to ride a bike

00:04:11.693 --> 00:04:13.458
and you've rode the bike
more and more and more,

00:04:13.458 --> 00:04:15.324
you became more skilled at it,

00:04:15.324 --> 00:04:17.274
you developed more
confidence in your ability

00:04:17.274 --> 00:04:18.634
to ride the bike.

00:04:18.634 --> 00:04:20.830
Any area of your life,
you're going to go into

00:04:20.830 --> 00:04:25.095
for the first time, be
okay with being uncertain.

00:04:25.095 --> 00:04:27.494
Be okay with being uncomfortable.

00:04:27.494 --> 00:04:29.877
Be okay with not knowing
how it's going to turn out.

00:04:29.877 --> 00:04:31.074
'Cause it's your first time.

00:04:31.074 --> 00:04:33.098
But stumble into it anyway.

00:04:33.098 --> 00:04:35.046
Because if you stumble
into it, you develop skill.

00:04:35.046 --> 00:04:38.186
You develop comfort with
it, and then something,

00:04:38.186 --> 00:04:39.427
one day, you wake up and say,

00:04:39.427 --> 00:04:40.542
I'm pretty confident doing this.

00:04:40.542 --> 00:04:42.543
Nothing changed in who you are.

00:04:42.543 --> 00:04:44.202
What changed is your level of competency,

00:04:44.202 --> 00:04:46.884
knowledge, skill, and ability.

00:04:46.884 --> 00:04:48.295
That's what competence is, knowledge,

00:04:48.295 --> 00:04:49.702
skill, ability and talent,

00:04:49.702 --> 00:04:52.636
And so when we finally
realize, oh, wow, I just need

00:04:52.636 --> 00:04:53.830
to learn a little bit more here,

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:54.940
I'll be more confident.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:56.806
I just need to apply this
a little bit more here,

00:04:56.806 --> 00:04:58.283
I'll be more confident.

00:04:58.283 --> 00:05:00.879
As you develop skills and
abilities in your life

00:05:00.879 --> 00:05:04.933
in your unique areas, you
become more confident.

00:05:04.933 --> 00:05:06.585
- Study the alchemy.

00:05:06.585 --> 00:05:08.048
Now here's what I mean by that.

00:05:08.048 --> 00:05:10.942
What happens to most people in life,

00:05:10.942 --> 00:05:14.783
especially the victims,
is they reach a part

00:05:14.783 --> 00:05:16.558
of their life, a difficult time,

00:05:16.558 --> 00:05:18.454
and they call it failure.

00:05:18.454 --> 00:05:22.178
I mean even just labeling
a setback as a failure

00:05:22.178 --> 00:05:24.781
triggers something into your mindset

00:05:24.781 --> 00:05:27.601
to release cortisol, the fear hormone,

00:05:27.601 --> 00:05:30.416
which blocks your best performance.

00:05:30.416 --> 00:05:33.323
And so you want to see
yourself and you want to study

00:05:33.323 --> 00:05:36.118
the alchemy, which is where
other people are seeing

00:05:36.118 --> 00:05:39.256
a failure or setback or
even a little problem

00:05:39.256 --> 00:05:40.987
you want to look for the gold.

00:05:40.987 --> 00:05:45.820
You want to train your
brain so you have a dominant

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:48.593
belief that just becomes
automatic because you've practiced

00:05:48.593 --> 00:05:50.741
it so much, where when someone
says there's a problem,

00:05:50.741 --> 00:05:54.124
or there's a wall, or there's a failure,

00:05:54.124 --> 00:05:58.291
you literally see the alchemy
to turn the lead into gold.

00:06:00.316 --> 00:06:01.723
Maybe you're going through a divorce,

00:06:01.723 --> 00:06:03.711
maybe you're going through an illness,

00:06:03.711 --> 00:06:05.613
a lot of people who watch this podcast

00:06:05.613 --> 00:06:08.759
are going through difficulties
or outright tragedies,

00:06:08.759 --> 00:06:12.176
but you want to practice
seeing the setback

00:06:13.595 --> 00:06:17.678
or the dark time as fuel
to leverage your growth.

00:06:19.137 --> 00:06:21.286
And so on this first point
about study the alchemy,

00:06:21.286 --> 00:06:23.825
no matter what you're
going through in your life,

00:06:23.825 --> 00:06:27.098
whether it's a large
difficulty or a small setback,

00:06:27.098 --> 00:06:29.487
you want to ask yourself, how can I use

00:06:29.487 --> 00:06:33.713
what I'm going through
as fuel for my growth?

00:06:33.713 --> 00:06:37.511
You want to ask yourself, what
is the gorgeous opportunity

00:06:37.511 --> 00:06:41.247
hidden inside this so-called problem?

00:06:41.247 --> 00:06:42.696
I mean and if you look
at the mass of humanity,

00:06:42.696 --> 00:06:45.518
they really train their
brains to focus on problems.

00:06:45.518 --> 00:06:48.293
Right, they hit a wall, and they just,

00:06:48.293 --> 00:06:50.579
because of their belief system,

00:06:50.579 --> 00:06:52.228
they don't know it, because
most people are really

00:06:52.228 --> 00:06:55.031
not that aware of their belief systems,

00:06:55.031 --> 00:06:56.526
so they blame it on the world.

00:06:56.526 --> 00:06:58.101
They blame it on the supplier.

00:06:58.101 --> 00:06:59.633
They blame it on the teammate.

00:06:59.633 --> 00:07:01.992
They blame it on their husband or wife.

00:07:01.992 --> 00:07:03.735
They blame it on their childhood.

00:07:03.735 --> 00:07:06.681
Versus understanding our daily performance

00:07:06.681 --> 00:07:08.666
reflects our deepest beliefs.

00:07:08.666 --> 00:07:11.141
And so if you are experiencing
some kind of setback,

00:07:11.141 --> 00:07:12.427
whether it's large or tiny,

00:07:12.427 --> 00:07:16.453
you want to train your
brain to see the treasure

00:07:16.453 --> 00:07:18.477
within the lead.

00:07:18.477 --> 00:07:20.560
- But if you want to
convey honest confidence

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:22.174
that inspires people, make
sure you can back it up.

00:07:22.174 --> 00:07:24.674
As much confidence as Ali had,

00:07:25.565 --> 00:07:28.174
and as much as he showed he
was just as good in the ring,

00:07:28.174 --> 00:07:29.995
but that wasn't everything that Ali had.

00:07:29.995 --> 00:07:33.058
He was also a dominant
force in every interaction.

00:07:33.058 --> 00:07:35.666
Watch here how he completely
ignores the reporter

00:07:35.666 --> 00:07:37.200
telling him that he isn't pretty

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:38.890
and delivers one of his most iconic lines.

00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:41.039
- I'm the king of the world.

00:07:41.039 --> 00:07:41.872
- Hold it, hold it, hold it.

00:07:41.872 --> 00:07:42.705
- I'm pretty.

00:07:42.705 --> 00:07:43.906
- Hold it, you're not that pretty.

00:07:43.906 --> 00:07:45.072
- I'm a bad man.

00:07:45.072 --> 00:07:46.361
I shook up the world.

00:07:46.361 --> 00:07:48.098
I shook up the world.

00:07:48.098 --> 00:07:49.958
- This kind of interaction
might look insignificant,

00:07:49.958 --> 00:07:52.475
but it happens all the time
and it is very important.

00:07:52.475 --> 00:07:54.209
It is a clash of frames.

00:07:54.209 --> 00:07:56.447
We've talked about this
before in different videos

00:07:56.447 --> 00:07:57.659
on tearing, if you want to check that out,

00:07:57.659 --> 00:08:00.029
but right here, Ali's frame
says that he should be

00:08:00.029 --> 00:08:00.862
doing the talking.

00:08:00.862 --> 00:08:03.068
The reporter's frame says
that Ali should listen

00:08:03.068 --> 00:08:04.307
because he's asking questions.

00:08:04.307 --> 00:08:06.584
Those two frames collide
and Ali, as always,

00:08:06.584 --> 00:08:08.314
comes out on top.

00:08:08.314 --> 00:08:11.001
The winner of any frame collision
is going to be the person

00:08:11.001 --> 00:08:13.612
who believes their frame
more, but watch here to see

00:08:13.612 --> 00:08:15.938
the subtleties of how
that is communicated,

00:08:15.938 --> 00:08:18.007
and I want you to pay
attention to three things.

00:08:18.007 --> 00:08:20.450
First, how Ali doesn't
look at the reporter,

00:08:20.450 --> 00:08:21.769
but the reporter looks at him.

00:08:21.769 --> 00:08:24.301
Second, how Ali doesn't respond
to what the reporter says,

00:08:24.301 --> 00:08:26.733
but the reporter responds to him.

00:08:26.733 --> 00:08:28.882
And third, how the reporter
tries to physically pull Ali

00:08:28.882 --> 00:08:31.945
towards him, but Ali doesn't budge.

00:08:31.945 --> 00:08:33.232
- I'm the king of the world.

00:08:33.232 --> 00:08:34.276
- Hold it, hold it, hold it.

00:08:34.276 --> 00:08:35.109
- I'm pretty.

00:08:35.109 --> 00:08:36.230
- Hold it, you're not that pretty.

00:08:36.230 --> 00:08:37.227
- I'm a bad man.

00:08:37.227 --> 00:08:38.719
I shook up the world.

00:08:38.719 --> 00:08:40.782
I shook up the world.

00:08:40.782 --> 00:08:42.349
- That took about eight
seconds, which is as long

00:08:42.349 --> 00:08:44.793
as it takes for there to be
a winner in a frame game.

00:08:44.793 --> 00:08:47.606
These short little frame
collisions happen all the time

00:08:47.606 --> 00:08:49.596
throughout your day, in
tons of different ways.

00:08:49.596 --> 00:08:51.824
And what's remarkable is
that from what I can tell,

00:08:51.824 --> 00:08:55.223
Muhammad Ali always stayed
firmly rooted in his own frame.

00:08:55.223 --> 00:08:57.663
When we see that kind of conviction,

00:08:57.663 --> 00:08:58.907
we can't help but be drawn to it.

00:08:58.907 --> 00:09:01.678
Here's just another little
example that you might relate to.

00:09:01.678 --> 00:09:04.580
When two people go to give
different styles of handshakes,

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:07.886
watch how Ali maintains his frame.

00:09:07.886 --> 00:09:09.122
(clapping)

00:09:09.122 --> 00:09:13.039
(environment drowns out voice)

00:09:18.974 --> 00:09:20.270
Ali isn't trying to be a jerk.

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:22.155
In fact, he was very playful
throughout this interview.

00:09:22.155 --> 00:09:24.696
But he has grown accustomed
to setting the tone

00:09:24.696 --> 00:09:25.727
of the interaction.

00:09:25.727 --> 00:09:28.206
Pay attention in your own
life in subtle little ways.

00:09:28.206 --> 00:09:29.938
Who do you make adjustments for,

00:09:29.938 --> 00:09:31.542
and who do you stick to your guns with?

00:09:31.542 --> 00:09:34.096
That's a pretty clear
indication of who the leader is

00:09:34.096 --> 00:09:35.703
in any sort of relationship.

00:09:35.703 --> 00:09:37.896
Now, there is no problem
with taking a follower role

00:09:37.896 --> 00:09:40.801
sometimes, but in certain
situations, like sales pitches,

00:09:40.801 --> 00:09:44.027
interviews, even dates, you
are usually going to benefit

00:09:44.027 --> 00:09:46.188
from being the leader.

00:09:46.188 --> 00:09:48.428
So those frame games
become very important.

00:09:48.428 --> 00:09:51.160
Ali was so confident,
had such a strong frame,

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:53.891
that he seemingly had
no fear of being honest.

00:09:53.891 --> 00:09:55.382
That might not seem like a big deal,

00:09:55.382 --> 00:09:57.788
but most people are extremely afraid

00:09:57.788 --> 00:09:59.269
of what others would think of them,

00:09:59.269 --> 00:10:00.801
if they were totally real.

00:10:00.801 --> 00:10:02.285
- I'm a social psychologist.

00:10:02.285 --> 00:10:04.932
I study prejudice, and I teach

00:10:04.932 --> 00:10:06.745
at a competitive business school.

00:10:06.745 --> 00:10:08.851
So it was inevitable that I would become

00:10:08.851 --> 00:10:10.840
interested in power dynamics.

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:14.306
I became especially interested
in non-verbal expressions

00:10:14.306 --> 00:10:16.458
of power and dominance.

00:10:16.458 --> 00:10:19.905
And what are non-verbal
expressions of power and dominance?

00:10:19.905 --> 00:10:21.887
Well, this is what they are.

00:10:21.887 --> 00:10:24.735
So in the animal kingdom,
they are about expanding,

00:10:24.735 --> 00:10:27.219
so you make yourself big, you stretch out,

00:10:27.219 --> 00:10:30.351
you take up space, you're
basically opening up.

00:10:30.351 --> 00:10:32.555
It's about opening up.

00:10:32.555 --> 00:10:35.281
And this is true across
the animal kingdom.

00:10:35.281 --> 00:10:36.893
It's not just limited to primates.

00:10:36.893 --> 00:10:39.826
And humans do the same thing.

00:10:39.826 --> 00:10:43.665
So they do this both when they have power,

00:10:43.665 --> 00:10:45.739
sort of chronically, and also
when they're feeling powerful

00:10:45.739 --> 00:10:47.220
in the moment.

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:49.012
And this one is especially interesting,

00:10:49.012 --> 00:10:52.243
because it really shows
us how universal and old

00:10:52.243 --> 00:10:55.102
these expressions of power are.

00:10:55.102 --> 00:10:57.530
This expression, which is known as pride,

00:10:57.530 --> 00:10:59.194
Jessica Tracy has studied.

00:10:59.194 --> 00:11:02.463
She shows that people
who are born with sight

00:11:02.463 --> 00:11:04.785
and people who are born congenitally blind

00:11:04.785 --> 00:11:07.488
do this when they win at
a physical competition.

00:11:07.488 --> 00:11:09.646
So when they cross the
finish line and they've won,

00:11:09.646 --> 00:11:11.854
it doesn't matter if they've
never seen anyone do it,

00:11:11.854 --> 00:11:13.209
they do this.

00:11:13.209 --> 00:11:15.998
So the arms up in the V,
the chin is slightly lifted.

00:11:15.998 --> 00:11:18.124
What do we do when we feel powerless?

00:11:18.124 --> 00:11:19.742
We do exactly the opposite.

00:11:19.742 --> 00:11:21.526
We close up.

00:11:21.526 --> 00:11:23.026
We wrap ourselves up.

00:11:23.026 --> 00:11:24.351
We make ourselves small.

00:11:24.351 --> 00:11:26.213
We don't want to bump into
the person next to us.

00:11:26.213 --> 00:11:29.905
So again, both animals and
humans do the same thing.

00:11:29.905 --> 00:11:32.506
And this is what happens
when you put together

00:11:32.506 --> 00:11:33.583
high and low power.

00:11:33.583 --> 00:11:36.352
So what we tend to do
when it comes to power

00:11:36.352 --> 00:11:38.886
is that we complement
the others' nonverbals.

00:11:38.886 --> 00:11:40.792
So if someone is being
really powerful with us,

00:11:40.792 --> 00:11:42.815
We tend to make ourselves smaller.

00:11:42.815 --> 00:11:45.005
We don't mirror them, we
do the opposite of them.

00:11:45.005 --> 00:11:47.919
So we know that our
nonverbals govern how other

00:11:47.919 --> 00:11:49.861
people think and feel about us.

00:11:49.861 --> 00:11:51.390
There's a lot of evidence,

00:11:51.390 --> 00:11:52.223
but our question really was

00:11:52.223 --> 00:11:54.246
do our nonverbals govern how we think

00:11:54.246 --> 00:11:56.001
and feel about ourselves?

00:11:56.001 --> 00:11:59.024
There's some evidence that they do.

00:11:59.024 --> 00:12:04.019
For example, when we, we
smile when we feel happy,

00:12:04.019 --> 00:12:06.260
but also when we're
forced to smile by holding

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:08.958
a pen in our teeth like this.

00:12:08.958 --> 00:12:10.248
It makes us feel happy.

00:12:10.248 --> 00:12:12.197
So it goes both ways.

00:12:12.197 --> 00:12:16.190
When it comes to power,
it also goes both ways.

00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:19.664
So when you feel powerful,
you're more likely to do this,

00:12:19.664 --> 00:12:23.331
but it's also possible
that when you pretend

00:12:25.055 --> 00:12:27.790
to be powerful you are more likely

00:12:27.790 --> 00:12:29.702
to actually feel powerful.

00:12:29.702 --> 00:12:31.902
We decided to bring people into the lab

00:12:31.902 --> 00:12:33.803
and run a little experiment,

00:12:33.803 --> 00:12:37.499
and these people adopted for two minutes

00:12:37.499 --> 00:12:40.606
either high power poses or low power poses

00:12:40.606 --> 00:12:43.308
and I'm just going to show
you five of the poses,

00:12:43.308 --> 00:12:45.133
although they took on only two.

00:12:45.133 --> 00:12:46.300
So here's one.

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:48.460
Couple more.

00:12:50.409 --> 00:12:54.012
This one has been dubbed the
Wonder Woman by the media.

00:12:54.012 --> 00:12:56.659
Here are a couple more.

00:12:56.659 --> 00:12:58.362
So you can be standing
or you can be sitting

00:12:58.362 --> 00:13:00.970
and here are the low power poses.

00:13:00.970 --> 00:13:05.306
So you're folding up, you're
making yourself small.

00:13:05.306 --> 00:13:06.879
This one is very low power,

00:13:06.879 --> 00:13:08.336
when you're touching
your neck, you're really

00:13:08.336 --> 00:13:09.961
kind of protecting yourself.

00:13:09.961 --> 00:13:12.186
So this is what happens.

00:13:12.186 --> 00:13:14.333
They come in, they spit into a vial.

00:13:14.333 --> 00:13:17.730
We, for two minutes say,
you need to do this or this.

00:13:17.730 --> 00:13:19.266
They don't look at pictures of the poses,

00:13:19.266 --> 00:13:20.797
we don't want to prime them
with a concept of power.

00:13:20.797 --> 00:13:23.647
We want them to be feeling power, right,

00:13:23.647 --> 00:13:25.176
so two minutes they do this.

00:13:25.176 --> 00:13:27.442
We then ask them how powerful do you feel

00:13:27.442 --> 00:13:29.134
on a series of items,
and then we give them

00:13:29.134 --> 00:13:31.235
an opportunity to gamble,

00:13:31.235 --> 00:13:34.257
and then we take another saliva sample.

00:13:34.257 --> 00:13:35.800
That's it, that's the whole experiment.

00:13:35.800 --> 00:13:37.295
So this is what we find.

00:13:37.295 --> 00:13:39.002
Risk tolerance, which is the gambling,

00:13:39.002 --> 00:13:41.454
what we find is that when
you're in the high power

00:13:41.454 --> 00:13:45.699
pose condition, 86% of you will gamble.

00:13:45.699 --> 00:13:48.970
When you're in the low power
pose position, only 60%,

00:13:48.970 --> 00:13:51.234
and that's a pretty whopping
significant difference.

00:13:51.234 --> 00:13:53.836
Here's what we find on testosterone.

00:13:53.836 --> 00:13:56.195
From their baseline when they come in,

00:13:56.195 --> 00:13:59.257
high power people experience
about a 20% increase

00:13:59.257 --> 00:14:03.424
and low power people experience
about a 10% decrease.

00:14:04.599 --> 00:14:07.008
So again, two minutes and
you get these changes.

00:14:07.008 --> 00:14:09.117
Here's what you get on cortisol.

00:14:09.117 --> 00:14:12.886
High power people experience
about a 25% decrease,

00:14:12.886 --> 00:14:17.438
and low people experience
about a 15% increase.

00:14:17.438 --> 00:14:19.668
So two minutes lead to
these hormonal changes

00:14:19.668 --> 00:14:22.432
that configure your brain to basically be

00:14:22.432 --> 00:14:25.958
either assertive,
confident, and comfortable,

00:14:25.958 --> 00:14:28.561
or really stress reactive.

00:14:28.561 --> 00:14:31.129
And feeling sort of shut down,

00:14:31.129 --> 00:14:33.128
and we've all had that feeling, right?

00:14:33.128 --> 00:14:37.057
So it seems that our nonverbals do govern

00:14:37.057 --> 00:14:39.119
how we think and feel about ourselves.

00:14:39.119 --> 00:14:40.848
So it's not just others,
but it's also ourselves.

00:14:40.848 --> 00:14:43.787
Also, our bodies change our minds.

00:14:43.787 --> 00:14:46.764
But the next question, of
course, is can power posing

00:14:46.764 --> 00:14:49.404
for a few minutes really change your life

00:14:49.404 --> 00:14:50.772
in meaningful ways?

00:14:50.772 --> 00:14:52.428
So this is in the lab,
it's this little task,

00:14:52.428 --> 00:14:55.442
it's just a couple of minutes,
where can you actually

00:14:55.442 --> 00:14:57.770
apply this, which we
cared about, of course.

00:14:57.770 --> 00:15:01.353
And so we think, it's
really, what matters,

00:15:02.417 --> 00:15:05.186
where you want to use this
is evaluative situations,

00:15:05.186 --> 00:15:07.248
like social threat situations.

00:15:07.248 --> 00:15:09.445
Where are you being evaluated,
either by your friends,

00:15:09.445 --> 00:15:12.171
like for teenagers, at
the lunchroom table.

00:15:12.171 --> 00:15:14.459
It could be for some people speaking

00:15:14.459 --> 00:15:16.411
at a school board meeting.

00:15:16.411 --> 00:15:19.908
It might be giving a pitch
or giving a talk like this

00:15:19.908 --> 00:15:22.314
or doing a job interview.

00:15:22.314 --> 00:15:25.492
We decided that the one that
most people could relate to,

00:15:25.492 --> 00:15:27.900
because most people had been
through was the job interview.

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:30.924
So we published these findings

00:15:30.924 --> 00:15:33.533
and the media are all
over it, and they say,

00:15:33.533 --> 00:15:35.645
okay, so this is what
you do when you go in

00:15:35.645 --> 00:15:36.800
for the job interview, right?

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:38.205
(laughing)

00:15:38.205 --> 00:15:40.608
So we were, of course,
horrified, and said, oh my god,

00:15:40.608 --> 00:15:42.470
no, no, no, no, that's
not what we meant at all,

00:15:42.470 --> 00:15:45.166
for numerous reasons, no,
no, no, don't do that.

00:15:45.166 --> 00:15:47.867
Again, this is not about
you talking to other people,

00:15:47.867 --> 00:15:49.112
it's you talking to yourself.

00:15:49.112 --> 00:15:51.031
What do you do before you
go into a job interview?

00:15:51.031 --> 00:15:52.602
You do this.

00:15:52.602 --> 00:15:54.423
Right, you're sitting down,
you're looking at your iPhone,

00:15:54.423 --> 00:15:56.901
or your Android, not
trying to leave anyone out.

00:15:56.901 --> 00:16:00.188
You are looking at your
notes, you're hunching up,

00:16:00.188 --> 00:16:02.219
making yourself small, when
really what you should be doing

00:16:02.219 --> 00:16:04.328
maybe, is this, like the bathroom, right?

00:16:04.328 --> 00:16:06.647
Do that, find two minutes.

00:16:06.647 --> 00:16:08.395
So that's what we want to test, okay?

00:16:08.395 --> 00:16:10.182
So we bring people into a lab,

00:16:10.182 --> 00:16:13.654
and they do either high power
or low power poses again,

00:16:13.654 --> 00:16:16.179
they go through a very
stressful job interview.

00:16:16.179 --> 00:16:19.829
It's five minutes long,
they are being recorded,

00:16:19.829 --> 00:16:21.694
they're being judged also,

00:16:21.694 --> 00:16:26.256
and the judges are trained to
give no nonverbal feedback.

00:16:26.256 --> 00:16:28.037
So they look like this.

00:16:28.037 --> 00:16:29.937
Imagine this is the
person interviewing you.

00:16:29.937 --> 00:16:32.087
So for five minutes, nothing.

00:16:32.087 --> 00:16:34.855
And this is worse than being heckled.

00:16:34.855 --> 00:16:36.648
People hate this.

00:16:36.648 --> 00:16:38.266
It's what Mary Ann La France calls

00:16:38.266 --> 00:16:40.214
standing in social quicksand.

00:16:40.214 --> 00:16:42.443
So this really spikes your cortisol.

00:16:42.443 --> 00:16:44.764
So this is the job interview
we put them through,

00:16:44.764 --> 00:16:47.205
'cause we really wanted
to see what happened.

00:16:47.205 --> 00:16:49.574
We then have these coders
look at these tapes,

00:16:49.574 --> 00:16:50.615
four of them.

00:16:50.615 --> 00:16:52.481
They're blind to the
hypothesis, they're blind

00:16:52.481 --> 00:16:54.850
to the conditions, they have
no idea who's been posing

00:16:54.850 --> 00:16:55.931
in what pose.

00:16:55.931 --> 00:16:59.893
And they end up looking
at these sets of tapes

00:16:59.893 --> 00:17:03.287
and they say, oh, we want
to hire these people,

00:17:03.287 --> 00:17:04.484
all the high power posers.

00:17:04.484 --> 00:17:06.050
We don't want to hire these people.

00:17:06.050 --> 00:17:09.280
We also evaluate these people
much more positively overall.

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:11.560
But what's driving it?

00:17:11.560 --> 00:17:14.211
It's not about the content of the speech,

00:17:14.211 --> 00:17:17.334
it's about the presence that
they're bringing to the speech.

00:17:17.334 --> 00:17:19.283
We also, because we rate
them on all these variables

00:17:19.283 --> 00:17:21.635
related to competence,
like how well structured

00:17:21.635 --> 00:17:24.327
is the speech, how good is
it, what other qualifications,

00:17:24.327 --> 00:17:26.186
no effect on those things.

00:17:26.186 --> 00:17:27.602
This is what's affected.

00:17:27.602 --> 00:17:29.135
These kinds of things.

00:17:29.135 --> 00:17:30.929
People are bringing their true selves.

00:17:30.929 --> 00:17:33.124
They're bringing themselves.

00:17:33.124 --> 00:17:35.113
They're bringing their
ideas, but as themselves,

00:17:35.113 --> 00:17:37.562
with no residue over them.

00:17:37.562 --> 00:17:39.796
So this is what's driving the effect

00:17:39.796 --> 00:17:42.123
or mediating the effect.

00:17:42.123 --> 00:17:45.596
So when I tell people
about this, that our bodies

00:17:45.596 --> 00:17:49.284
change our minds and our
minds can change our behavior

00:17:49.284 --> 00:17:50.901
and our behavior can change our outcomes,

00:17:50.901 --> 00:17:54.318
they say to me, I don't,
it feels fake, right?

00:17:54.318 --> 00:17:55.567
So I said, fake it 'til you make it,

00:17:55.567 --> 00:17:58.542
like I don't, it's not me,
like I don't want to get there

00:17:58.542 --> 00:18:00.950
and then still feel like a fraud.

00:18:00.950 --> 00:18:02.405
I don't want to feel like an impostor.

00:18:02.405 --> 00:18:03.938
I don't want to get there, only to feel

00:18:03.938 --> 00:18:06.262
like I'm not supposed to be here.

00:18:06.262 --> 00:18:09.212
And that really resonated
with me because I want to

00:18:09.212 --> 00:18:12.225
tell you a little story
about being an impostor

00:18:12.225 --> 00:18:13.669
and feeling like I'm
not supposed to be here.

00:18:13.669 --> 00:18:17.679
When I was 19, I was in a
really bad car accident.

00:18:17.679 --> 00:18:20.805
I was thrown out of a
car, rolled several times,

00:18:20.805 --> 00:18:23.869
I was thrown from the car,
and I woke up in a head

00:18:23.869 --> 00:18:27.344
injury rehab ward, and I had
been withdrawn from college.

00:18:27.344 --> 00:18:30.427
And I learned that my IQ had dropped

00:18:32.183 --> 00:18:33.876
by two standard deviations.

00:18:33.876 --> 00:18:37.020
Which was very traumatic, I knew my IQ

00:18:37.020 --> 00:18:38.883
because I had identified with being smart,

00:18:38.883 --> 00:18:40.880
and I had been called gifted as a child.

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:43.609
So taken out of college,
I keep trying to go back,

00:18:43.609 --> 00:18:45.642
they say you're not
going to finish college.

00:18:45.642 --> 00:18:48.964
Like, just there are other
things for you to do,

00:18:48.964 --> 00:18:50.575
but that's not going to work out for you.

00:18:50.575 --> 00:18:54.055
So I really struggled with
this, and I have to say,

00:18:54.055 --> 00:18:57.362
having your identity taken
from you, your core identity,

00:18:57.362 --> 00:18:59.036
and for me it was being smart,

00:18:59.036 --> 00:19:01.279
having that taken from you,
there's nothing that leaves

00:19:01.279 --> 00:19:03.505
you feeling more powerless than that.

00:19:03.505 --> 00:19:06.689
So I felt entirely powerless,
I worked and worked and worked

00:19:06.689 --> 00:19:08.510
and I got lucky and worked
and got lucky and worked.

00:19:08.510 --> 00:19:10.781
Eventually I graduated from college.

00:19:10.781 --> 00:19:13.581
Took me four years longer than my peers.

00:19:13.581 --> 00:19:16.807
And I convinced someone, my angel advisor,

00:19:16.807 --> 00:19:21.131
Susan Fisk, to take me on, and
so I ended up at Princeton,

00:19:21.131 --> 00:19:23.364
and I was like, I am
not supposed to be here.

00:19:23.364 --> 00:19:25.065
I am an impostor.

00:19:25.065 --> 00:19:27.051
And the night before my first year talk,

00:19:27.051 --> 00:19:28.753
and the first year talk at
Princeton is a 20 minute talk

00:19:28.753 --> 00:19:31.331
to 20 people, that's it.

00:19:31.331 --> 00:19:33.984
I was so afraid of being
found out the next day

00:19:33.984 --> 00:19:37.335
that I called her and said, I'm quitting.

00:19:37.335 --> 00:19:39.406
She was like, you are not quitting.

00:19:39.406 --> 00:19:41.517
Because I took a gamble
on you and you're staying.

00:19:41.517 --> 00:19:44.003
You're going to stay and this
is what you're going to do.

00:19:44.003 --> 00:19:45.536
You are going to fake it.

00:19:45.536 --> 00:19:47.397
You're going to take, you're
going to do every talk

00:19:47.397 --> 00:19:49.018
that you ever get asked to do.

00:19:49.018 --> 00:19:50.719
You're just going to do
it and do it and do it,

00:19:50.719 --> 00:19:52.460
even if you're terrified,

00:19:52.460 --> 00:19:55.685
and just paralyzed and having
an out of body experience,

00:19:55.685 --> 00:19:57.703
until you have this moment where you say,

00:19:57.703 --> 00:20:01.013
oh my gosh, I'm doing
it, I have become this.

00:20:01.013 --> 00:20:02.463
I am actually doing this.

00:20:02.463 --> 00:20:03.492
So that's what I did.

00:20:03.492 --> 00:20:04.698
Five years in grad school.

00:20:04.698 --> 00:20:06.820
Few years, I'm at Northwestern,
I moved to Harvard,

00:20:06.820 --> 00:20:10.584
I'm at Harvard, I'm not really
thinking about it anymore

00:20:10.584 --> 00:20:12.246
but for a long time I had been thinking,

00:20:12.246 --> 00:20:13.978
not supposed to be here,
I'm not supposed to be here.

00:20:13.978 --> 00:20:17.228
So the end of my first year at Harvard,

00:20:19.325 --> 00:20:20.571
a student who had not talked in class

00:20:20.571 --> 00:20:22.481
the entire semester, who I had said,

00:20:22.481 --> 00:20:25.051
look, you got to participate
or else you're going to fail,

00:20:25.051 --> 00:20:27.412
came into my office, I really
didn't know her at all,

00:20:27.412 --> 00:20:31.310
and she said, she came
in, totally defeated,

00:20:31.310 --> 00:20:34.810
and she said, I'm not supposed to be here,

00:20:38.284 --> 00:20:40.806
and that was the moment for me,

00:20:40.806 --> 00:20:42.133
because two things happened.

00:20:42.133 --> 00:20:44.116
One was that I realized oh my gosh,

00:20:44.116 --> 00:20:45.484
I don't feel like that anymore.

00:20:45.484 --> 00:20:48.096
You know, I don't feel
that anymore, but she does,

00:20:48.096 --> 00:20:50.036
and I get that feeling and the second was,

00:20:50.036 --> 00:20:51.278
she is supposed to be here.

00:20:51.278 --> 00:20:53.641
Like she can fake it, she can become it.

00:20:53.641 --> 00:20:55.790
So I was like, yes, you are.

00:20:55.790 --> 00:20:57.824
You are supposed to be
here, and tomorrow you're

00:20:57.824 --> 00:20:59.883
going to fake it, you're going
to make yourself powerful,

00:20:59.883 --> 00:21:01.332
and you know.

00:21:01.332 --> 00:21:03.582
(clapping)

00:21:09.141 --> 00:21:10.917
And you're going to go into the classroom

00:21:10.917 --> 00:21:13.412
and you are going to give
the best comment ever.

00:21:13.412 --> 00:21:16.974
And she gave the best comment ever

00:21:16.974 --> 00:21:18.842
and people turned around
and they were like,

00:21:18.842 --> 00:21:20.130
oh my god, I didn't even
notice her sitting there.

00:21:20.130 --> 00:21:22.862
She comes back to me months later,

00:21:22.862 --> 00:21:25.639
and I realize that she
had not just faked it

00:21:25.639 --> 00:21:27.581
till she made it, she'd actually faked it

00:21:27.581 --> 00:21:29.443
till she became it.

00:21:29.443 --> 00:21:30.839
So she had changed.

00:21:30.839 --> 00:21:34.933
And so I want to say to you
don't fake it till you make it.

00:21:34.933 --> 00:21:36.964
Fake it till you become it.

00:21:36.964 --> 00:21:40.105
It's not, do it enough
until you actually become it

00:21:40.105 --> 00:21:41.186
and internalize.

00:21:41.186 --> 00:21:43.101
The last thing I want to
leave you with is this.

00:21:43.101 --> 00:21:46.101
Tiny tweaks can lead to big changes.

00:21:48.235 --> 00:21:50.760
So this is two minutes.

00:21:50.760 --> 00:21:53.072
Two minutes, two minutes, two minutes.

00:21:53.072 --> 00:21:55.471
Before you go into the next
stressful evaluative situation,

00:21:55.471 --> 00:21:58.352
for two minutes, try
doing this in the elevator

00:21:58.352 --> 00:22:01.887
in a bathroom stall, at your
desk behind closed doors.

00:22:01.887 --> 00:22:02.888
That's what you want to do.

00:22:02.888 --> 00:22:05.273
Get, configure your brain to cope the best

00:22:05.273 --> 00:22:06.726
in that situation.

00:22:06.726 --> 00:22:07.797
Get your testosterone up,

00:22:07.797 --> 00:22:09.088
get your cortisol down.

00:22:09.088 --> 00:22:10.899
Don't leave that situation feeling like,

00:22:10.899 --> 00:22:12.641
oh, I didn't show them who I am.

00:22:12.641 --> 00:22:14.964
Leave that situation
feeling like, oh, I really

00:22:14.964 --> 00:22:17.445
feel like I got to say who
I am and show who I am.

00:22:17.445 --> 00:22:21.612
So I want to ask you first,
both to try power posing

00:22:23.065 --> 00:22:27.281
and also I want to ask
you to share this science,

00:22:27.281 --> 00:22:28.524
'cause this is simple.

00:22:28.524 --> 00:22:30.132
I don't have ego involved in this.

00:22:30.132 --> 00:22:32.576
Give it away, like share it with people,

00:22:32.576 --> 00:22:34.525
because the people who can use it the most

00:22:34.525 --> 00:22:38.257
are the ones with no
resources and no technology.

00:22:38.257 --> 00:22:40.608
And no status and no power.

